Use the existing authentication provider and security architecture.

### Security requirements

- Never store plaintext passwords.
- Never expose password hashes, access tokens, refresh tokens, or private authentication credentials.
- Never trust a client-supplied user ID as the authorization boundary.
- Verify permissions on the backend or through trusted database policies.
- Prevent unauthorized profile and role changes.
- Validate authentication state consistently.
- Handle expired and invalid sessions safely.
- Prevent private pages from exposing stale sensitive data after logout.
- Follow the provider's established recovery and verification workflow.
- Avoid leaking sensitive authentication details in error responses.

Do not replace a working authentication provider with a custom authentication implementation.

Do not disable security checks to make tests pass.

